 brunt of mother  nature's wrath president biden plans to  visit the state  this week to assess the crisis jennifer  johnson  global news washington still ahead the  vatican's new view  on vaccines  one year after italy's first case of  coppa 19 a memorial service was held at  a church in cadonio  to mark the anniversary the town was one  of many communities that locked down for  weeks early last year during early  attempts to stop the virus from surging  out of control  since then italy has seen 2.8 million  cases  and over 95 000 deaths vatican city is  easing back from a decree on coven 19  vaccination  in the face of heavy criticism earlier  this month the head of the city-states  government suggested employees who  refuse to get vaccinated  without a valid medical reason could be  fired  as redmond shannon reports the  controversy reflects a difficult dilemma  faced by employers around the world  ash wednesday in a pandemic  strict social distancing inside saint  peter's basilica  with only pope francis allowed to go  without a mask  the same event in 2020 saw huge crowds  and just a few masks a reminder of the  tragedy  that was soon to befall italy  earlier this month the pontiff stressed  the importance of global fraternity to  help  fight covet 19.  along with vaccinations he has been a  vocal supporter of equal access to shots  across the world even opening doors at  the holy see to give homeless people  the vaccine the pope's words came on a  day when the head of the vatican  government  cardinal giuseppe bertello issued a  decree on vaccines  it implied that any vatican employee who  refused a vaccine without a legitimate  health reason  could be fired it sparked a backlash in  italy  the vatican later clarifying that no one  would lose their job  i suspect that the impulse for this um  came from pope francis  if not directly it's certainly  indirectly this  notion of care for all of humanity  theologian michael atridge says the push  for vatican vaccines could be more about  the wider flock particularly amid  concerns that  some vaccine research uses cell lines  replicated from aborted fetuses  catholics around the world will also be  looking to  the pope and other church leaders to  know what to do  around vaccinations it's difficult to  balance out  sort of the rights of individuals  individual rights and freedoms on the  one hand  and the common good that dilemma is  starting to become apparent to employers  across the world mandating the vaccine  where it is not mandated countrywide  is close to impossible giving incentives  rather than threats  could also be tricky but you can't turn  around and say that those who are  vaccinated get to come into work  and those who are not have to stay home  and there's more perks and benefits  available to those who are vaccinated  that's called prejudicial treatment  ottawa and the provinces  say covert 19 vaccines will remain  voluntary  at least for now redmond shannon global  news london  farmers in india aren't letting up on  their protests against changes to the  country's agriculture laws  police in punjab state say more than a  hundred thousand people  attended this rally the farmers argue  the new laws won't guarantee them a  minimum price for their crops  the government of prime minister  narendra modi insists the policy changes  are needed to deregulate the agriculture  industry  and modernize the economy the standoff  has persisted for more than six months  up next finding the balance between the  fishing industry and an endangered  species  there are fewer than 400 north atlantic  right whales  in the world and in canada federal  efforts to protect them are proving  successful  over a five-year period 25 of the  majestic mammals died  after becoming entangled in fishing gear  or being struck by vessels  last year there were none ross lord  looks at the restrictions that balances  saving the whales  and allowing the fishing industry to  keep doing business  when